<div class="slide" data-document-role="page" data-label="Hook">
<img class="photo" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Hook photo"><div class="shade"></div><div class="grad-bottom"></div>
<div class="counter">1/10</div>
<div class="safe bottom"><div class="kicker">What the research says</div><h1>The best time to work on meltdowns is when there <em>isn't</em> one.</h1><p class="body">Research shows that practicing emotional regulation skills during calm moments is one of the most effective ways to reduce future meltdowns.</p></div></div>
<div class="slide" data-document-role="page" data-label="Why it matters">
<img class="photo" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Why it matters photo"><div class="shade"></div><div class="grad-bottom"></div>
<div class="counter">2/10</div>
<div class="safe bottom"><div class="kicker">Why calm moments matter</div><h2>A flooded nervous system can't learn a new skill. A calm, connected one <em>can</em>.</h2><p class="body">In the storm, the thinking brain goes quiet. Calm moments and connection are where the practice actually sticks.</p></div></div>
<div class="slide" data-document-role="page" data-label="Try this">
<img class="photo" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Try this photo"><div class="shade"></div><div class="grad-top"></div>
<div class="counter">3/10</div>
<div class="safe top"><div class="kicker">Try this together</div><h2>Magic Reveal Art</h2><ul class="list"><li>White oil pastel</li><li>White cardstock</li><li>Spray bottle of water</li><li>Bleeding tissue paper, cut into squares</li></ul></div></div>
<div class="slide" data-document-role="page" data-label="Step 1">
<img class="photo" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Step 1 photo"><div class="shade"></div><div class="grad-top"></div>
<div class="counter">4/10</div>
<div class="safe top"><div class="kicker">Step 01</div><h2>Write a secret word or draw a hidden picture.</h2><p class="body">Use a white oil pastel on white cardstock. It stays invisible for now.</p></div></div>
<div class="slide" data-document-role="page" data-label="Step 2">
<img class="photo" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Step 2 photo"><div class="shade"></div><div class="grad-top"></div>
<div class="counter">5/10</div>
<div class="safe top"><div class="kicker">Step 02</div><h2>Spritz the cardstock with water.</h2><p class="body">A light mist across the whole page is all you need.</p></div></div>
<div class="slide" data-document-role="page" data-label="Step 3">
<img class="photo" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Step 3 photo"><div class="shade"></div><div class="grad-top"></div>
<div class="counter">6/10</div>
<div class="safe top"><div class="kicker">Step 03</div><h2>Cover the page with tissue squares.</h2><p class="body">Bleeding tissue paper releases its color when it gets wet. Overlap the squares until the page is covered.</p></div></div>
<div class="slide" data-document-role="page" data-label="Step 4">
<img class="photo" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Step 4 photo"><div class="shade"></div><div class="grad-top"></div>
<div class="counter">7/10</div>
<div class="safe top"><div class="kicker">Step 04</div><h2>Spray a little more, then let it dry.</h2><p class="body">Dry tissue peels off so much easier.</p></div></div>
<div class="slide" data-document-role="page" data-label="Step 5">
<img class="photo" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Step 5 photo"><div class="shade"></div><div class="grad-top"></div>
<div class="counter">8/10</div><img class="inset" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Finished reveal"><div class="inset-tag">The reveal</div>
<div class="safe top"><div class="kicker">Step 05</div><h2>Peel it off and watch the message appear.</h2><p class="body">The color soaks into everything except the spots the oil pastel protected.</p></div></div>
<div class="slide" data-document-role="page" data-label="Connection">
<img class="photo" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Connection photo"><div class="shade"></div><div class="grad-top"></div>
<div class="counter">9/10</div>
<div class="safe top"><div class="kicker">The real magic</div><h2>Make it about <em>connection</em>.</h2><p class="body">Write a secret message for your child, or have them draw a specific feeling for you. Then swap papers and reveal each other's surprise.</p></div></div>
<div class="slide" data-document-role="page" data-label="CTA">
<img class="photo" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="CTA photo"><div class="shade"></div><div class="grad-bottom"></div>
<div class="counter">10/10</div>
<div class="safe bottom"><div class="kicker">Save for later</div><h2>Save this for your next calm afternoon.</h2><p class="body">Follow <strong>@as_the_littles_grow</strong> for more ways to build regulation through connection.</p><p class="creds">Speech Pathologist + Highly Sensitive Person + Homeschooling Mom of 4</p></div></div>
<div class="slide caption-page" data-document-role="page" data-label="Caption (copy/paste)">
<div class="caption-flag">Caption: not for posting</div>
<div class="caption-body">The best time to work on meltdowns is when there isn't one.

It feels backwards. We want the skills to show up in the hard moment. But a flooded nervous system can't learn anything new. The learning happens in the calm, connected moments in between.

So this week, try a little magic. A secret word in white oil pastel, a page of bleeding tissue paper, a few sprays of water, and a slow peel to see what appears.

Then make it about the two of you. Write a hidden message for your child, or have them draw a feeling for you, swap papers, and reveal each other's surprise.

That's regulation practice, too. It just looks like art.

Save this for your next calm afternoon.</div>
